   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises to adopt new business models, technology and techniques to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity to provide entrepreneurship trainings:
A network of 70 trainers from 20 public, semi-private and private organizations started to provide quality start-up and business management training services to potential entrepreneurs and established businesses to grow, using the ILO&#x2019;s Start and Improve your Business (SIYB) training programme.  So far, over 300 IDPs, refugees and host communities were trained.



Increased capacity to provide financial education training:
A network of 46 trainers from 34 public and private partner organizations have started to provide quality financial education services to women and men, entrepreneurs, youth, refugees and IDPs. 

Increased capacity to provide adequate financial services: Partner banks and financial institutions adopted a newly developed standard operating procedure (SOP) aimed to unify/facilitate and expand access of financial services to end clients through all partner banks and Iraqi company for banking guarantees (ICBG), central bank of Iraq. 18 staff from three partner banks and Iraqi company for banking guarantees-ICBG (Financial institutions) are better able to serve to serve Iraqi SMEs and entrepreneurs through improved credit analysis skills

Increased capacity to provide early entrepreneurship education: 
The Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs has adopted and disseminated the Know about Business (KAB) entrepreneurship education program in the KRI. Under the Ministry&#x2019;s supervision, 57 KAB teachers in 6 selected Technical and Vocational Training Centers in the KRI have expanded KAB programs at schools and centers targeting youth and adults. Entrepreneurship trainings:
ILO adapted SIYB training materials to the Iraqi/Kurdish context in Arabic, and translated them into Kurdish Sorani and Badini. ILO then organized three training of trainers targeting business services providers to enable them to offer quality business support services. One Refresher SIYB training of trainers targeting already-certified trainers was organised in the KRI and federal territory to upgrade their knowledge. Management workshops targeting managers from 20 organizations and business development services providers were organized to enhance their organizational capacity to market, provide or sell, manage, monitor and improve SIYB. ILO Iraq Country Programme also contributed to the digitalization of the SIYB Program in order to enable BDS to provide remote services.
Financial education:
ILO adapted and translated the global Financial Education training package to the Iraqi/Kurdish context and translate them into Arabic, Kurdish Sorani and Badini. ILO then organized three training of trainers workshops on financial education for a total of 46 trainers from 34 partner institutions 
Financial services:
ILO developed a financial inclusion strategy to boost access to finance in collaboration with the Central Bank of Iraq, and organized a launching event under the patronage of the central bank of Iraq and with the participation of a wide spectrum of national and international stakeholders. ILO organized consultations and provided technical support to partner banks and financial institutions to develop a standard operating procedure for the processing of loan product to unify/facilitate access to financial services for targeted clients. ILO conducted a workshop to strengthen the capacity of selected financial institutions and the Iraqi company for banking guarantees on credit appraisals and proper credit analysis. 

Early entrepreneurship education:
The ILO adapted and translated the latest version of the KAB package.</narrative>
   </description>
